@meta
id: 10-22
title: Scenario — the status panel
chapter: 10
type: scenario
skills: obj-tag, obj-quote, obj-paren, obj-brace, obj-paragraph, obj-word, inner-vs-around
par: 200
minutes: 16
vscode: two files, nine selections, and a lot of squinting at brackets
open: OrderStatusPanel.tsx status.ts Badge.tsx

@brief
A ticket, and the last thing in this chapter.

    ORD-607  The order page still renders its own status pill instead of
             the shared Badge, the labels are missing CANCELLED, and the
             "(live)" note next to the heading has been wrong since
             polling was removed.

Three files are open. `:wn` writes the current file and moves on, `:wqa`
writes everything and quits. Work in the order they open.

  1. OrderStatusPanel.tsx

     - the section's class name becomes `order-status-panel`
     - the `(live)` note next to the heading goes, tags and all. The
       heading keeps the word `Status`
     - the status line renders the label, not the raw enum, so its
       contents become `{statusLabel(order)}`
     - the hand-rolled pill is replaced by the shared component:
       `<Badge status={order.status} />`

  2. status.ts

     - the label list gains `'Cancelled'` on the end
     - `formatStatus` now takes the whole order, so the fallback call
       passes `order` and nothing else
     - `legacyStatusLabel` goes. The legacy column was dropped in the
       same migration as the batch job, and nothing imports it.

  3. Badge.tsx

     The shared component. Read it if you want to check the prop name.
     Do not change it.

Nine edits. Every one of them is an object you have met in this chapter,
and none of them needs a selection.

@keys
| `ci"` | a class name |
| `dat` `cat` | an element, tags included |
| `cit` | an element's contents |
| `ci(` | an argument list |
| `ci[` | an array |
| `dap` | a function with a blank line round it |
| `:wn` `:wqa` | next file, and finish |

@hints
Read each edit and name the delimiter before you press anything. Quotes,
tags, round, square. The shape of the text picks the object for you.
--
Two of the JSX edits look similar and are not: one element is being
deleted outright and one is being swapped for a different element. `dat`
and `cat`.
--
`f(` lands inside the `(live)` note; `f{` lands inside the braces of the
status line and of the pill. In status.ts, `f[` for the array and `f(`
for the call, and the dead function is the last paragraph in the file.

@solution
    5j fp ci" order-status-panel <Esc>
    j 0 f( dat
    j 0 f{ cit {statusLabel(order)} <Esc>
    j 0 f{ cat <Badge status={order.status} /> <Esc>
    :wn<Enter>
    2j f[ ci[ 'Pending', 'Shipped', 'Cancelled' <Esc>
    3j 0 f( ci( order <Esc>
    3j dap
    :wqa<Enter>

About a hundred and seventy keystrokes, and something like a hundred and
twenty of them are text you had to type because it is new code. The
editing itself — nine structural changes across two files — came to
roughly fifty.

That ratio is the thing to take out of the chapter. Before text objects,
a session like this is mostly navigation and selection: find the closing
tag, find the closing bracket, drag, check, undo, drag again. After them,
almost all of your keystrokes are the words you meant to write, and the
structural work is three keys at a time.

The judgement is what is left, and it is worth naming because it is the
part that stays useful:

  - is the thing I am changing *inside* a delimiter, or is it the
    delimited thing itself? That picks `i` or `a`.
  - what is the delimiter? Quote, bracket, brace, tag, blank line.
  - am I replacing it or removing it? That picks `c` or `d`.

Three questions, and you can answer all three from looking at the screen.
`ci"` and `ci(` will be most of your text-object use forever; `cit`,
`dap` and the counted forms are the ones that feel like a superpower the
first few times, and then just feel normal.

Chapter 11 is the multiplier. `.` repeats your last change, and an edit
built out of a text object is exactly the kind of change that repeats
cleanly — which is why `ciw` plus `.` ends up being the rename you reach
for more often than a search and replace.

@start OrderStatusPanel.tsx
import { Badge } from '../components/Badge';
import { formatDate } from '../format';

export function OrderStatusPanel({ order }: Props) {
  return (
    <section className="status-panel">
      <h2>Status<small>(live)</small></h2>
      <p className="status-line">{order.status}</p>
      <span className="pill pill-legacy">{order.channel}</span>
      <footer>Updated {formatDate(order.updatedAt)}</footer>
    </section>
  );
}

@expected OrderStatusPanel.tsx
import { Badge } from '../components/Badge';
import { formatDate } from '../format';

export function OrderStatusPanel({ order }: Props) {
  return (
    <section className="order-status-panel">
      <h2>Status</h2>
      <p className="status-line">{statusLabel(order)}</p>
      <Badge status={order.status} />
      <footer>Updated {formatDate(order.updatedAt)}</footer>
    </section>
  );
}

@start status.ts
import { formatStatus } from './format';

export const STATUS_LABELS = ['Pending', 'Shipped'];

export function statusLabel(order: Order) {
  return STATUS_LABELS[order.status] ?? formatStatus(order.status, 'en-GB');
}

export function legacyStatusLabel(order: Order) {
  return order.legacyStatus === 'D' ? 'Shipped' : 'Pending';
}

@expected status.ts
import { formatStatus } from './format';

export const STATUS_LABELS = ['Pending', 'Shipped', 'Cancelled'];

export function statusLabel(order: Order) {
  return STATUS_LABELS[order.status] ?? formatStatus(order);
}

@start Badge.tsx
type Props = {
  status: OrderStatus;
};

export function Badge({ status }: Props) {
  return <span className={`badge badge-${status.toLowerCase()}`}>{status}</span>;
}
